  "methods": [
    {
      "name": "queryAndAggregateUsageStats",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "options",
          "type": {
            "k": "ref",
            "name": "UsageStatsOptions"
          },
          "optional": false
        }
      ],
      "returns": {
        "k": "ref",
        "name": "QueryAndAggregateUsageStatsResultMap"
      },
      "promise": true,
      "comment": "Queries and aggregates usage stats for the given time range.\n\nAndroid reads pre-aggregated daily/weekly/monthly/yearly buckets and sums\nevery bucket that intersects `[beginTime, endTime)`, without clipping to it.\n`totalTimeInForeground` can therefore include usage from outside the window.\nUse `queryUsageStats` for the unmerged per-interval buckets, or `queryEvents`\nfor timestamped lifecycle events in the requested range."
    },
    {
      "name": "queryUsageStats",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "options",
          "type": {
            "k": "ref",
            "name": "QueryUsageStatsOptions"
          },
          "optional": false
        }
      ],
      "returns": {
        "k": "ref",
        "name": "QueryUsageStatsResult"
      },
      "promise": true,
      "comment": "Queries usage stats for the given interval type and time range.\n\nWraps Android `UsageStatsManager.queryUsageStats`. Unlike\n`queryAndAggregateUsageStats`, this does not merge buckets: the result is\none `UsageStats` object per package per overlapping interval. Android may\nexpand `[beginTime, endTime)` to the nearest whole interval period, so\n`totalTimeInForeground` can include usage from outside the window.\n\nOn Android R (API 30) and above, the OS returns no data while the user is\nlocked; this plugin then resolves `{ stats: [] }`, which must not be treated\nas zero foreground usage.\n\nThis uses the same `PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS` permission as\n`queryAndAggregateUsageStats`."
    },
    {
      "name": "queryEvents",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "options",
          "type": {
            "k": "ref",
            "name": "QueryEventsOptions"
          },
          "optional": false
        }
      ],
      "returns": {
        "k": "ref",
        "name": "QueryEventsResult"
      },
      "promise": true,
      "comment": "Queries the raw usage event log for the given time range.\n\nReturns lifecycle events whose timestamps fall in `[beginTime, endTime)`.\nAndroid does not emit a synthetic event for \"already in foreground at\nbeginTime\" or \"still in foreground at endTime\". To measure duration across\nthose boundaries, pass an earlier `beginTime` as lookback and clip locally:\ntreat an unmatched pause as starting at the window start, and an unmatched\nresume as ending at the window end.\n\nAndroid retains events for only a few days. Older ranges may return an\nincomplete or empty list; that is not the same as zero foreground usage.\nOn Android R (API 30) and above, the OS also returns no events while the\nuser is locked; this plugin then resolves `{ events: [] }`, which likewise\nmust not be treated as zero foreground usage.\n\nCallers can sum resumed-to-paused intervals from the returned events.\nDo not treat `DEVICE_SHUTDOWN` as a pause: it is a reset marker, not an\ninterval close. This uses the same `PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS` permission as\n`queryAndAggregateUsageStats`.\n\nOnly lifecycle events are returned, to keep the bridge payload small:\n- `1` — ACTIVITY_RESUMED / MOVE_TO_FOREGROUND\n- `2` — ACTIVITY_PAUSED / MOVE_TO_BACKGROUND\n- `23` — ACTIVITY_STOPPED\n- `26` — DEVICE_SHUTDOWN (device-wide reset marker; still returned when\n  `packageName` is set. Android typically reports package `\"android\"`.\n  `packageName` is omitted if the OS does not attach one. The timestamp\n  is the last UsageStats persist before shutdown, not the actual\n  power-off. Open resume events without a matching pause between this\n  marker and the next boot have unknown duration and must be ignored.)"
    },
    {
